00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.340
Fran: Early intervention, I suppose we
can look at it in lots of different ways

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.200
because early intervention might be a bit
later in life in some respects, I think

00:00:12.189 --> 00:00:15.669
you know, early intervention, if we talk
about when people are first talking about

00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:19.689
suicidal feelings, instead of waiting
for people to, to be at the point where

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:24.604
they need to be hospitalized to just talk
about that and put in safety plans with

00:00:24.604 --> 00:00:28.394
them and talk about what they can do if
they do start to feel those feelings.

00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:30.344
I think that, I think
it has a massive effect.

00:00:30.344 --> 00:00:34.844
I think just having an idea of where
you can go and not feeling so lost

00:00:34.844 --> 00:00:36.194
with those feelings can be massive.

00:00:36.274 --> 00:00:38.944
Tamzin: I've had really good
outcomes from young people when

00:00:38.944 --> 00:00:40.714
they've been referred by early help.

00:00:40.744 --> 00:00:44.194
And it's been like something
quite like easy to work with.

00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:47.794
So it's been like this young person
wants to work on their anxiety.

00:00:48.064 --> 00:00:51.179
This young person wants to work
on, you know, their stress and

00:00:51.181 --> 00:00:54.511
I've found that those young people
have been ready for the support.

00:00:54.851 --> 00:00:57.241
I had one just recently like that - I
just finished with her about a month

00:00:57.241 --> 00:01:01.171
ago - and she was like, non-school
attender, she worked with me last year,

00:01:02.011 --> 00:01:03.841
now going into school four days a week.

00:01:03.991 --> 00:01:06.821
When I started working with her she
was just not even doing any days.

00:01:07.201 --> 00:01:11.221
And then obviously wasn't engaging
with the school counselor, now

00:01:11.221 --> 00:01:13.751
that I finished my support, she's
engaging with school counselor.

00:01:14.311 --> 00:01:18.341
The friendships at school was
limited, now she's trying to

00:01:18.361 --> 00:01:19.321
build up a friendship group.

00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:23.491
So again, early intervention with
her was key because she's still,

00:01:23.741 --> 00:01:24.991
she was, I think she was 13.

00:01:24.991 --> 00:01:25.951
So year nine is it?

00:01:26.731 --> 00:01:30.051
So she's still got quite a few years
of schooling to go and the GCSEs.

00:01:30.391 --> 00:01:34.231
So with her that was really good outcomes
because early help was involved, we

00:01:34.231 --> 00:01:37.361
had the debt relief involved with the
family, 'cause they mismanaged their

00:01:37.381 --> 00:01:39.901
finances and we also had school support.

00:01:39.901 --> 00:01:43.261
So it was really good outcomes
to get in early with her and do

00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:45.091
all those 12 sessions of support.

00:01:45.361 --> 00:01:47.911
And then at the end, you know,
she'd come right up and she was

00:01:48.001 --> 00:01:49.831
obviously back attending school more.

00:01:50.331 --> 00:01:52.461
Fran: I think the big thing
with an intervention for me.

00:01:52.551 --> 00:01:56.191
And I find this with a lot of school
age children that I work with, is that

00:01:56.191 --> 00:02:00.371
sometimes it's just learning about what
those things they're experiencing are.

00:02:00.371 --> 00:02:04.361
So, young people get told that they're
suffering from anxiety or depression, and

00:02:04.531 --> 00:02:07.531
then they come to me or one of the big
ones is they get told that they're angry

00:02:07.681 --> 00:02:11.311
-  they're an angry young man - or they're
just, they've got an anger problem.

00:02:11.401 --> 00:02:13.981
And they come to me and they're
like, "oh, I'm an angry person".

00:02:14.521 --> 00:02:17.211
And yeah, another lad I've worked
with, so he was coming to me at 14 and

00:02:17.211 --> 00:02:18.481
telling me he's an angry young man.

00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:21.391
So I just talked to him about what
anger is and where it comes from.

00:02:21.391 --> 00:02:23.311
And that nobody's
actually an angry person.

00:02:23.378 --> 00:02:27.831
But anger's like a secondary emotion that
comes from, you know, your experiences

00:02:27.831 --> 00:02:29.871
or things that are going on in your life.

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:34.821
So talk to me about understanding those
feelings of, you know, why you might

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:38.301
get angry sometimes, why you might throw
things around sometimes and, and what

00:02:38.301 --> 00:02:42.111
might be leading to that was life-changing
for him in some respects, because he

00:02:42.111 --> 00:02:45.531
just, he just thought he was an angry
person, and that was going to be how he

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:46.941
was going to be for the rest of his life.

00:02:46.971 --> 00:02:50.496
But to see that, well, no, your anger
is coming from a place and your anger

00:02:50.496 --> 00:02:53.203
is you know, there's other things you
can do instead of the things you do

00:02:53.203 --> 00:02:56.293
when you're angry, there's things we can
teach you to learn coping mechanisms.

00:02:56.983 --> 00:03:01.783
You know, he, he had a sort of total
breakthrough with that work and, you

00:03:01.783 --> 00:03:02.873
know, things changed in the home.

00:03:02.923 --> 00:03:04.033
Things changed at school.

00:03:04.623 --> 00:03:06.073
Things changed with his partner.

00:03:06.141 --> 00:03:09.101
Yeah, so that was, that was a
massive example of early intervention

00:03:09.361 --> 00:03:17.171
really playing a role I think.

